'''''Chrysojasminum fruticans''''' (syn. ''Jasminum fruticans''), known as the '''wild jasmine''' or '''common yellow jasmine''', is a species of flowering plant in the family [[Oleaceae]], native to the Mediterranean region and the Middle East as far as northern Iran.<ref name="POWO_77144221-1" >{{cite POWO |id=77144221-1 |title=''Chrysojasminum fruticans'' (L.) Banfi |access-date=8 June 2023 }}</ref> It is the only native [[jasmineae]] species in Europe.<ref name="auto">{{Cite journal |last=Puyoou |first=Aurore |last2=Gryta |first2=Hervé |last3=Fuchs |first3=Anne-Laure |last4=Blanchard |first4=Pierrick |last5=Cheptou |first5=Pierre-Olivier |last6=Civeyrel |first6=Laure |last7=Dufay |first7=Mathilde |last8=Dupin |first8=Julia |last9=Jargeat |first9=Patricia |last10=Lecompte |first10=Emilie |last11=Besnard |first11=Guillaume |date=2023-08-17 |title=Limitation of gene flow by distance in the common yellow jasmine (''Chrysojasminum fruticans'', Oleaceae): implications for the study of its mating strategies |url=https://doi.org/10.1093/botlinnean/boad035 |journal=Botanical Journal of the Linnean Society |volume=204 |issue=1 |pages=23–34 |doi=10.1093/botlinnean/boad035 |issn=0024-4074}}</ref> It is a semi-evergreen shrub with yellow flowers and black berries.<ref name="RHS_12384" >{{cite web |title=''Jasminum fruticans'' common yellow jasmine |url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/54316/jasminum-fruticans/details |date=2023 |publisher=The Royal Horticultural Society |access-date=9 June 2023 |quote=Other common names; make-bate ... 8 suppliers }}</ref>

== Description ==
Alternate, trifoliate leaves and yellow flowers, about 15&nbsp;mm long.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Banfi |first=Enrico |date=2014-08-01 |title=Chrysojasminum, a new genus for Jasminum sect. Alternifolia (Oleaceae, Jasmineae) |url=https://doi.org/10.4081/nhs.2014.54 |journal=Natural History Sciences |volume=1 |issue=1 |pages=3 |doi=10.4081/nhs.2014.54 |issn=2385-0922|doi-access=free }}</ref><ref name=":0">{{Cite journal |last=Guitián |first=Javier |last2=Guitián |first2=Pablo |last3=Medrano |first3=Monica |date=June 1998 |title=Floral biology of the distylous Mediterranean shrub Jasminum fruticans (Oleaceae) |url=https://nsojournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1756-1051.1998.tb01870.x |journal=Nordic Journal of Botany |language=en |volume=18 |issue=2 |pages=195–201 |doi=10.1111/j.1756-1051.1998.tb01870.x |issn=0107-055X|url-access=subscription }}</ref> The flowers are typically in clusters at the end of the branches, 2 to 4 flowers per cluster.<ref name=":1">{{Citation |last=Bussmann |first=Rainer W. |chapter=Chrysojasminum fruticans (L.) Banif. Oleaceae |date=2025 |title=Ethnobotany of Mountain Regions |pages=1561–1565 |chapter-url=https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-91369-3_597 |access-date=2026-04-26 |place=Cham |publisher=Springer Nature Switzerland |isbn=978-3-031-91368-6 |last2=Paniagua-Zambrana |first2=Narel Y.}}</ref> The flowers produce nectar and they have been reported to last 3 days. The plan fruits in autumn; the berry is small (0.5 - 0.8&nbsp;cm in diameter), round and black.<ref name=":0" /><ref name=":1" /><ref name="auto"/>

=== Floral Morphology & Reproduction ===
This species is known to display [[heterostyly]], with two different morphs: short-styled (short style & long stamens) & long-styled (long style & short stamens).<ref name=":0" /><ref name=":2">{{Cite journal |last=Thompson |first=John D. |last2=Dommée |first2=Bertrand |date=2000-11-27 |title=Morph‐specific patterns of variation in stigma height in natural populations of distylous ''Jasminum fruticans'' |url=https://doi.org/10.1046/j.1469-8137.2000.00765.x |journal=New Phytologist |volume=148 |issue=2 |pages=303–314 |doi=10.1046/j.1469-8137.2000.00765.x |issn=0028-646X}}</ref>

[[File:Distylie_jasminum_fruticans.svg|alt=A diagram showing the reproductive parts of both flower morphs.|Distylie jasminum fruticans]]

The plant commonly spreads clonally, through its rhizomes. The plants are self-incompatible (cannot self pollinate) and within-morph incompatible (pollination within the same morph does not produce viable seed).<ref name=":2" />

== Uses ==
The wild jasmine is commonly used as an ornamental shrub.<ref name=":1" /> It is listed as non-toxic for cats and dogs.<ref>{{Citation |last=Means |first=Charlotte |chapter=Appendix 3: Plants Not Reported to be Toxic |date=2025-06-19 |title=Small Animal Toxicology Essentials |pages=373–379 |editor-last=Wismer |editor-first=Tina |chapter-url=https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/9781394180141.app3 |access-date=2026-04-26 |edition=1 |publisher=Wiley |language=en |doi=10.1002/9781394180141.app3 |isbn=978-1-394-18011-0|doi-access=free }}</ref>

== Ecology ==
This species is typically found in [[calcareous]] soils.<ref name=":0" />

[[Red deer]] showed a high preference for this species in a foraging study.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Paton |first=D. |last2=Nuñez |first2=J. |last3=Bao |first3=D. |last4=Muñoz |first4=A. |date=October 2002 |title=Forage biomass of 22 shrub species from Monfragüe Natural Park (SW Spain) assessed by log–log regression models |url=https://doi.org/10.1006/jare.2001.0993 |journal=Journal of Arid Environments |volume=52 |issue=2 |pages=223–231 |doi=10.1006/jare.2001.0993 |issn=0140-1963|url-access=subscription }}</ref> [[Iberian ibex]]es have also been found feeding on this species.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Martinez |first=Teodora |last2=Martinez |first2=Encarnación |last3=Fandos |first3=Paulino |date=1985-12-20 |title=Composition of the food of the Spanish Wild Goat in Sierras de Cazorla and Segura, Spain |url=https://doi.org/10.4098/at.arch.85-31 |journal=Acta Theriologica |volume=30 |pages=461–494 |doi=10.4098/at.arch.85-31 |issn=0001-7051}}</ref>

Wild jasmine is visited by a wide range of pollinators, including [[Hymenoptera]] (such as bees), [[Fly|Diptera]] (such as bee-flies) and [[Lepidoptera]] (such as hawkmoths). No difference was found in the visitation rate of pollinators based on the flower morph.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Thompson |first=J. D. |date=1 February 2001 |title=How do visitation patterns vary among pollinators in relation to floral display and floral design in a generalist pollination system? |url=https://doi.org/10.1007/s004420000531 |journal=Oecologia |volume=126 |issue=3 |pages=386–394 |doi=10.1007/s004420000531 |issn=0029-8549|url-access=subscription }}</ref>

Paper wasps (''[[Polistes gallicus]]'') have been found to nest in wild jasmines.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=PEREZ-BOTE |first=Jose Luis |last2=MORA-RUBIO |first2=Carlos |last3=LOPEZ MARTINEZ |first3=Josefa |last4=RODRIGUEZ RIANO |first4=Tomas |date=2020-05-13 |title=Nesting ecology of Polistes gallicus (Hymenoptera: Vespidae) in South-Western Spain |url=https://doi.org/10.14411/eje.2020.026 |journal=European Journal of Entomology |volume=117 |pages=243–251 |doi=10.14411/eje.2020.026 |issn=1210-5759|doi-access=free }}</ref> Wild jasmines are one of the three host species of the olive leaf moth, ''[[Palpita vitrealis|Palpita unionalis]]''.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Kumral |first=N. A. |title=Life tables of the olive leaf moth Palpita unionalis (Hübner)(Lepidoptera: Pyralidae), on different host plants |url=https://acikerisim.uludag.edu.tr/entities/publication/b3cde1f0-1d53-4371-81bf-8eaeb704112f |journal=Journal of Biological and Environmental Sciences |volume=1 |issue=3}}</ref>

== Cultural Significance ==

* The wild jasmine is mentioned as a medicinal plant in one of [[Al-Biruni]]'s works.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Tayjanov |first=Karimjon |last2=Khojimatov |first2=Olim |last3=Gaffrov |first3=Yusufjon |last4=Makhkamov |first4=Trobjon |last5=Bussmann |first5=Rainer W |last6=Normakhamatov |first6=Nodirali |date=2021-12-25 |title=Plants and fungi in the ethnomedicine of the medieval East - a review |url=https://doi.org/10.32859/era.22.46.1-20 |journal=Ethnobotany Research and Applications |volume=22 |doi=10.32859/era.22.46.1-20 |issn=1547-3465|doi-access=free }}</ref>
* The plant appears in 9 traditional songs of [[Castile and León|Castilla y Leon]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Cardaño |first=M. |last2=Herrero |first2=Baudilio |date=2014-11-24 |title=Plants in the Songbooks of Castilla y León, Spain |url=https://doi.org/10.17348/era.12.0.535-549 |journal=Ethnobotany Research and Applications |volume=12 |pages=535 |doi=10.17348/era.12.0.535-549 |issn=1547-3465|doi-access=free }}</ref>
* The flowers are a motif in Turkish traditional silk based needlework.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Dogan |first=Yunus |title=Plant patterns of silk based needlework, a traditional handcraft in Turkey |url=https://d1wqtxts1xzle7.cloudfront.net/38041440/2010-IJTK-Plant_patterns_of_silk_based_needlework-libre.pdf?1435619466=&response-content-disposition=inline%3B+filename%3DPlant_patterns_of_silk_based_needlework.pdf&Expires=1777238937&Signature=d3CAGy3i2fanCR6eGlw807nLzmUbdXG69Kwu6xSKx0nlPnT8~Wt8Gi9FsSOfLmhmyCYUwOoHUnH2zxe2VAH4HhW5XCfaxWOgSee~r0dkrFvkz9kRJ7I62J5B4ILHHNQq2KGiL~nRxfdIKNQksBGwcH73zZ6mJQE6-7dclgEcSQ8hNuIJt40sUWw~VyRC6q8NCZp1eOrqzxzuf1ajtXXt1MhhF~Uj3QeHEQpj4M3VkqJwbN~3aKAZxJhoJkb0rzgqkw0ybxFRejbWinLLOQzFXfpMuzjZ51Xrn8KDyESB4G5DxA05FGuvAgcCV2RCaEbj-vSEYCghC06ZlAyrXbx9EA__&Key-Pair-Id=APKAJLOHF5GGSLRBV4ZA |journal=Indian Journal of Traditional Knowledge |volume=9 |issue=4}}</ref>

